WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.984
A run passes only when its final submission

2
00:00:03.047 --> 00:00:05.169
beats the human reference on every

3
00:00:05.232 --> 00:00:06.667
metric the task checks

4
00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:08.540
and on every hidden workload.

5
00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.263
The criteria are set from the reference's

6
00:00:11.321 --> 00:00:12.304
own measurements

7
00:00:12.304 --> 00:00:14.330
and some of them ask for a margin.

8
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.600
Let's look at one task's rule.

9
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.996
SVG-EAR is about a video model's

10
00:00:20.084 --> 00:00:21.580
sparse attention.

11
00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.930
Its rule has four parts.

12
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.691
The latency must be at most zero point

13
00:00:26.754 --> 00:00:28.531
nine six of the reference's

14
00:00:28.594 --> 00:00:30.308
so the run must be at least

15
00:00:30.371 --> 00:00:31.640
four percent faster.

16
00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.037
And three measures of image quality

17
00:00:34.096 --> 00:00:36.026
must stay within small margins of

18
00:00:36.085 --> 00:00:37.430
the reference's scores.

19
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.267
PSNR may be at most zero point

20
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.340
two five decibels lower.

21
00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:45.027
SSIM may be at most zero

22
00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:47.170
point zero one lower.

23
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.040
And LPIPS may be at most zero

24
00:00:50.127 --> 00:00:52.560
point zero zero five higher.

25
00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.019
Here is a check of our own on one workload

26
00:00:56.019 --> 00:00:58.095
with just two of the measures

27
00:00:58.095 --> 00:00:59.300
latency and PSNR.

28
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.825
The reference has a latency of one hundred

29
00:01:02.825 --> 00:01:04.450
and a PSNR of thirty.

30
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.487
The first candidate has a

31
00:01:06.554 --> 00:01:08.173
latency of ninety-seven

32
00:01:08.173 --> 00:01:10.400
and a PSNR of thirty point four.

33
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.317
The second has a latency of ninety

34
00:01:13.317 --> 00:01:16.030
and a PSNR of twenty-nine point two.

35
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.100
The third has a latency of ninety-five

36
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.820
and a PSNR of twenty-nine point eight.

37
00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.330
Pause and predict: which of them pass?

38
00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:27.927
The bounds are a latency

39
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.380
of at most ninety-six

40
00:01:29.444 --> 00:01:31.592
and a PSNR of at least twenty-nine

41
00:01:31.656 --> 00:01:32.730
point seven five.

42
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.062
The first candidate is three percent faster

43
00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:38.840
but the rule asks for four, so it fails.

44
00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:41.537
The second is ten percent faster

45
00:01:41.537 --> 00:01:43.741
but it loses too much quality

46
00:01:43.741 --> 00:01:44.950
so it fails too.

47
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.860
Only the third passes both checks.

48
00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.646
Faster is not enough

49
00:01:49.646 --> 00:01:51.833
the gain must clear the margin

50
00:01:51.902 --> 00:01:53.884
and the quality may drop only

51
00:01:53.953 --> 00:01:55.730
as far as the rule allows.

52
00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.453
A task counts every such check

53
00:01:58.524 --> 00:02:00.310
on every hidden workload.

54
00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.759
SVG-EAR has twelve clips

55
00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:05.109
with four measures on each

56
00:02:05.109 --> 00:02:07.460
that is forty-eight checks.

57
00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.296
As of October twenty-sixth

58
00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:12.965
no model has passed SVG-EAR

59
00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:14.530
zero of sixteen.

60
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.277
In the case study's twelve runs

61
00:02:17.277 --> 00:02:19.600
the closest was GPT-6 Astra's.

62
00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.950
It passed forty-three of them.

63
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:23.890
Here is the trap.

64
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.475
The final score averages over every

65
00:02:26.539 --> 00:02:28.200
metric and every workload

66
00:02:28.264 --> 00:02:30.052
so a large gain in one place

67
00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:31.840
can hide a loss in another.

68
00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.505
On KV Compaction

69
00:02:33.505 --> 00:02:36.213
Claude Opus 5.5 has the top score

70
00:02:36.213 --> 00:02:39.230
and it sits above the human reference.

71
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.536
But its answers fall short of the

72
00:02:41.595 --> 00:02:43.373
reference's accuracy on two of

73
00:02:43.432 --> 00:02:45.980
the twenty-four articles, so the run fails.

74
00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:48.635
The benchmark wants a result the paper's

75
00:02:48.693 --> 00:02:50.479
authors would accept as better

76
00:02:50.537 --> 00:02:51.920
not a gain on one metric

77
00:02:51.977 --> 00:02:53.130
bought with another.

78
00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:56.130
So, a pass needs every metric on

79
00:02:56.206 --> 00:02:57.870
every hidden workload.

80
00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.300
Some rules ask for a margin.

81
00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.046
And the average score can hide a loss

82
00:03:03.109 --> 00:03:05.001
so a score above the reference

83
00:03:05.064 --> 00:03:06.010
can still fail.

84
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.333
As an exercise

85
00:03:07.398 --> 00:03:10.058
change the latency margin from zero point

86
00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:12.329
nine six to zero point nine eight

87
00:03:12.329 --> 00:03:14.600
and see which candidates pass now.

88
00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:17.590
Next: how to read the leaderboard.
